## Method note -- why frame capture, not `show mirror`
`show mirror` CANNOT be used as proof on this platform. Defect M2 below shows it reporting a
direction the dataplane is not using. Every verdict here rests on what tcpdump saw on eth3.
`cap.sh` counts echo REQUESTS (what the DUT RECEIVES on port1.0.48) against echo REPLIES (what
the DUT TRANSMITS out of port1.0.48). That split is what distinguishes transmit from receive.

## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- D0 baseline, no mirror configured
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                            <-- empty
host# ./cap.sh d0        (../work-box/cap-d0.txt, cap-d0.err, ping-d0.txt)
  cap-d0.err:  0 packets captured / 0 packets received by filter / 0 packets dropped by kernel
  ping-d0.txt: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  PASS. Traffic flows end to end and eth3 sees none of it. The destination port is silent
  before any mirror exists, so anything it captures later is attributable to the mirror.

### STEP 1 -- "set the mirror direction to transmit" and read it back
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction transmit
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: transmit                <-- direction as set
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48           <-- source as named
x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.48
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: transmit
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48           <-- the command keys on the MONITORED port and
                                                returns the same record
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   mirror interface port1.0.48 direction transmit
  => STEP 1 PASS: the CLI accepts the command on the DESTINATION port naming the SOURCE port,
     `show mirror` and `show mirror interface <source>` both report destination port1.0.10,
     direction transmit and monitored port1.0.48, and the line is in the running-config.

### STEP 2 -- frame-level proof that `transmit` mirrors ONLY egress
host# ./cap.sh tx        (../work-box/cap-tx.txt, cap-tx.err, ping-tx.txt)
  cap-tx.txt:
    08:55:01.300457 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 1, length 64
    08:55:01.830767 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 2, length 64
    08:55:02.342621 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 3, length 64
    08:55:02.854758 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 4, length 64
    08:55:03.366611 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 5, length 64
    08:55:03.878738 IP 10.38.22.70 > 10.38.22.65: ICMP echo reply, id 25748, seq 6, length 64
  cap-tx.err:  6 packets captured / 6 packets received by filter / 0 packets dropped by kernel
  ping-tx.txt: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  counted: echo REQUESTS (ingress on 1.0.48) = 0 ; echo REPLIES (egress on 1.0.48) = 6
  => STEP 2 PASS: six replies and ZERO requests. The replies are what the DUT TRANSMITS out of
     port1.0.48 towards the host; the requests are what it RECEIVES on that port. Only the
     transmitted direction was copied, which is exactly what `direction transmit` means. The
     ping still passed 0% loss, so mirroring did not disturb normal forwarding. This is positive
     proof: six frames that could only appear on eth3 if the mirror were working, and the
     absence of the other direction is the discriminator, not merely an absence of error.

### STEP 3 -- D2 removal
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.48
  % port1.0.10 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
      <-- ADVISORY, not a rejection. The command SUCCEEDED and the prompt advanced.
          tools/cfg.py printed "(advisory, not an error -- continuing)" and carried on; before
          fix d301d72 it would have aborted the batch and silently skipped the rest.
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                            <-- empty
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access                   <-- mirror line gone, port back to a plain access port
host# ./cap.sh after-rm  (../work-box/cap-after-rm.txt, .err, ping-after-rm.txt)
  cap-after-rm.err: 0 packets captured / 0 packets received by filter
  ping-after-rm.txt: 6 packets transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  => STEP 3 PASS: the `no` form is accepted, the line is gone from the running-config, and eth3
     captures nothing again while the traffic still flows. Removal is complete in both the
     configuration and the dataplane.

## Defects observed (not part of this case's step text; see T44968 for the full evidence)
M1  A mirror RE-APPLIED to a destination port that has previously held one does not take effect
    until the destination port is bounced. `show mirror` reports it correctly meanwhile.
M2  `mirror interface <src> direction <new>` issued while a mirror already exists on that
    destination is SILENTLY IGNORED -- no error, and `show mirror` keeps reporting the OLD
    direction. `no mirror interface <src>` must be issued first.
Neither affects this verdict: this case configured the first mirror on a port that had never
been a destination, which works immediately, and it was read back and proven frame by frame.

VERDICT: PASS -- `mirror interface port1.0.48 direction transmit` is accepted on the destination
port, reported correctly by both `show mirror` and `show mirror interface port1.0.48`, and
copies ONLY the frames the DUT transmits out of the monitored port (6 replies, 0 requests)
without disturbing forwarding; the `no` form removes it cleanly from config and dataplane.
